      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was officially platted in 1836, just a year after a post office opened there in 1835.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashley%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ashley sits on Route 161, about six miles south of Bowling Green, making it a quiet stop along the highway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashley%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "William Ashley’s 1820 appointment as lieutenant governor marked the first time a fur trader held that office in Missouri.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/bica/learn/historyculture/william-ashley.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The post office that served Ashley for 130 years closed its doors in 1965, ending a long chapter of local communication.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashley%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community’s early growth was driven by frontier men who answered an advertisement, a group that included many notable figures of the era.",
